WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
Desert Commander, the enemy has been cited.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.400
Your challenge has been accepted.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:10.100
The future of the free world now awaits your brave and steady command.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:33.620
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.460
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.080
And that's it again.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.900
Although we did get word from Joe, he misses you all and he hopes to be back next episode.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.940
And he got you all little magnets that have like beach sand in them.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.800
Yeah, he traveled the entire world actually and stopped at every single country and got magnets.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.940
And we hope to, you know, make those a subscriber bonus at some point.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.860
We don't plan to do any.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.720
No. We don't plan to do much, but we do plan to have Joe back if he'll if he'll welcome us with open arms.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.740
Yeah, there's still that one thing to work out.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.900
But I think we could sign the contract by the end of the week.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.980
Great. And, you know, we're talking about Desert Commander today.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.720
Sean, turn-based strategy video game on the NES.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.040
Were you expecting it?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.420
Did you think they could pull it off?

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.980
You know, I was definitely expecting it to happen at some point.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:35.760
I wasn't expecting this to be it or like it to happen so soon, even though, you know, 180 whatever games isn't really that soon.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.180
But no, I was very excited to find to hear that this was a turn-based strategy game.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.920
And you're kind of a turn-based strategy guy like this is this is one of your preferred genres.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.200
Am I wrong? Yeah, I'm I'm really into XCOM.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:02:01.360
I kind of I kind of dug the Advanced Wars games, even though I didn't play too much of them.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:11.060
And, you know, like even though it's a different genre, I do very much prefer turn-based RPGs than I do real-time RPGs.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.760
So, yeah, I'm I dig this format.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.820
And what is it that excites you about turn-based RPGs?

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:28.640
Because I think some people find them to just be a little too slow, maybe a little too similar to like chess, you know, where you have to like you're doing a lot more thinking than playing.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.900
Are those the things that excite you or is it something else?

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.820
Yeah, I mean, there are definitely games where I I prefer that like, yes, this is about twitch reflexes.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.600
And this is about how you perform in the moment.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:03:02.040
But it's almost a bit more of a calm and relaxing challenge to to have a to have a turn-based strategy game where you do have that time to say, like, this is why each of these other turns will fail or will sort of work against me.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.800
And this is the least bad solution I can think of.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.240
I kind of like that that mental that mental sequence.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:24.340
Yeah, I'd argue I even like the more recent trend of turn-based strategy games that just flat out tell you what the most likely result to be is based on the units that you're comparing to another.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:40.080
So like, you know, I know Fire Emblem does it, but even, you know, a lot of, you know, more strategic war games that come out today, they'll tell you like, you know, you have a 94% chance of victory in this one particular battle between your tanks and their infantry, you know?

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.920
And it's like, some people hate that because it takes the, you know, the element of surprise out of the video game.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:58.840
But I would argue that in a strategy game, I want to be playing as like the most dominant force that I that I think I could be, you know, I want to know what the odds are against me and plan accordingly.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:09.940
Yeah, with like more modern games that aren't that aren't in this genre per se, like, like Into the Breach, which I guess is pretty much this.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:20.060
And also Slay the Spire, that which is very much not in this genre, but takes a lot of like turn-based strategy cues.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:28.240
Like they've all sort of borrowed from that same feel of like what a turn-based game is.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:34.460
And like, yeah, they give you pretty much perfect information and you have to work with that perfect information.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:48.360
That's like a whole theory of strategy games of like is perfect information just giving you like a perfect way to win or is it another way to play?

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.600
And I guess like that that sort of doesn't make sense the way I phrased it.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:57.960
But I like that more modern takes on on these turn-based games as well.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:04.000
And a lot of turn-based strategy games just come down to like the setting too, if nothing else.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:14.040
Like that is what makes a lot of these games different other than maybe like, you know, one core mechanic that like they add on to addition of all the usual turn-based strategy tropes.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.960
And I guess what I'm getting at here is like, you know, this is just, you know, war.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.620
This is war in the desert essentially.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.880
You know, there's no like fun video game-centric units here.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.540
It's all going for what I would imagine at the time to be very realistic.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.280
Obviously, you know, no more does this have a realistic effect on it.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:44.180
But, you know, that's what they've chosen here and you could also do it in space or you could do it, you know, in like a Game of Thrones fantasy timeline.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.880
Like you can do it through all different kinds of settings.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:52.260
What did you think about the, you know, World War II desert aesthetic here?

00:05:52.260 --> 00:06:08.620
Well, one, I did know that this game in the other localizations had a bit more personality in that like you were the allied forces fighting against Axis forces.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:17.180
Whereas in this American version, you are just two teams of soldiers fighting in the desert.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:27.040
And while like when you have that context, the desert makes sense and there's like some interesting flavor going on.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:34.440
Now that you've removed the actual historical context, now this whole desert environment is kind of bland.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:45.360
And I think that if they did just like, I'm kind of getting ahead of myself here, but I would have preferred more map variety.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.300
Because there are different maps.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.240
They're just very similar.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.420
Yeah, you almost can't tell that there's different maps throughout the game.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:07:05.640
And maybe that's just a problem with their, you know, with their setting here of, I think, you know, as you were saying, because of in the Japanese version, it was taking place in North Africa, I think.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:16.220
There's just, there's just something about World War II, though, that is kind of just like timeless to these war strategy games.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:26.080
In a lot of ways, people who never played a turn-based strategy game before, you know, before 1989, where this might have been their introduction, I feel like they would have gotten it out of risk.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:31.660
You know, they could see the parallels there between the board game Risk and this game.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:33.800
Yeah, I guess so.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:35.060
I didn't play too much Risk.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.180
I played a few games and was not great at it.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:40.880
It's a little bit more abstracted.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:49.280
This game is a very abstracted war game, but Risk even more so.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:50.420
But you're right.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:59.840
It has enough similarities that it could be seen as like an entry to at least give your brain some context as to how to approach this thing.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:07.120
Right. And, of course, you know, the main gameplay objective here is not total domination over your enemy.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.940
You have to take over their enemy headquarters.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.680
Destroying all the opposing forces is fine.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.340
Yeah, you will win.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:21.860
Yeah, you will win that way, but you would ultimately still have to take out their king or the enemy headquarters, if you will.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:23.460
A king, of course, referring to the chess board.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.380
Yeah, there you are with the chess.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.060
Yeah, sorry. No, we'll see.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:33.740
And, you know, I think what's interesting there is there's, you know, there's a way to do that, right?

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.980
To say, like, you know, the objective here is destroy enemy headquarters.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.360
If enemy headquarters was like a thing on the map, I could understand that being the objective.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:53.140
But this is like enemy headquarters is just another unit on the map that is not necessarily all that more stronger than any of the other particular units.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.380
Like, yeah, it can move a little better and it can fight off all different types of enemies.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:03.600
But at the end of the day, it is just subject to the same things that all units in the game are subject to.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.720
And therefore, it makes the objective a little easier to exploit.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:09.680
Would you agree with that, Sean?

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:19.840
Well, yeah. Okay. So, like, if we were playing a multiplayer version of this game, like me versus you, I think it would make a lot more sense.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:30.880
Like, the fact that the computer in this game, the AI is a little rudimentary, it is easy to exploit.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:41.360
Like, you just have to bait some units away from the king unit or, like, you know, like the captain or the base, whatever you want to call it.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:50.800
But just so that you have enough time to surround it with, I don't know, a couple bombers and maybe some infantry if you can get them there with the truck.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.180
And it doesn't matter how many units they have left.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:57.980
You basically just surrounded their general and you win.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:10.280
But I think when you think of it like that and you think of the game mechanics more and how they would perform in a two-player setting, it does make sense.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:23.400
Like, because if you want to think about it in, like, the sense of what this is trying to simulate of the base basically being, like, just your command ops.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:33.720
Like, whoever is in the field giving command and structure to your forces, like, they would be kind of mobile.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:40.860
And maybe they aren't all that strong and you wouldn't ask that group to take out a battalion of tanks.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:46.800
But you would want it to be somewhat mobile and you would want to protect it.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:11:00.060
So I think if me and you played, we would be much more conservative in how we played with the base and would make it harder for each of us to actually win this game.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:15.040
And it seems like in a lot of these one-player scenarios, the only way that they can make it harder for you to win the game is just by piling on more and more units to fight against.

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:44.360
Yeah, that's a good point, Sean, because there are five different scenarios.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:45.240
Yeah.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:52.660
That they provide to you and they just change basically from being beginner, easy, hard, all-star, if you will.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.360
You know, like, they just continue to move up a little bit.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.260
But you're right. They're not really challenging.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:01.940
It's not like the computer is getting more processing power and therefore making smarter plays.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:07.560
The difference between the scenarios is, you know, your placements on the map.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:15.300
Sometimes they'll try to spread your units out across like, you know, instead of having all your units together, you'll have some on the east side and some on the south side.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:21.680
But otherwise, it all just has to do with the number of units your computer opponent has.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:25.820
That's how they make it harder, by just giving him more units than you.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:36.340
And I think that's also telling to the fact that they probably saw the game as something that was easy to exploit, you know, through playtesting and decided that that would be the way to handle it.

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:47.660
The funny thing is, I'm sure you didn't try this, but you can actually, similar to how a two-player game is played in this game, you can just plug in a controller into player two.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:53.180
And on that screen with the computer, you can remove units from the computer side.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:54.480
I did learn that.

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:56.700
And make it a fair fight.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:58.780
That is, it's very silly.

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:00.760
I would never want to do that.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:11.420
But I think turn-based strategy games are the only genre that I will, like, be serious about in Iron Man mode, where, like, oh, I can't just save scum.

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:19.380
So that would be a cardinal sin for me to do that and fuck with their units.

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.340
But, no, I did learn that you could do that.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.400
It's kind of silly.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:28.580
I like what you talked about save scumming there for a second, because it's a very obvious point, too, though.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:32.120
But, like, I guess, you know, nothing's stopping anybody from doing that.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:47.180
But this might be the one, like, you know, or not the only genre, but it might be the most important genre that you can't do something like that, because ultimately you're better off just watching somebody else play the game.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.020
You're seeing the result of the battle take place.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:14:01.040
Saying you don't like it and rewinding or save stating and trying it again is, you know, is literally defeating the purpose of playing through the map.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:02.440
So I agree with you there.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:17.100
And that's why I also agree with, like, not changing the units that the computer has, because clearly, you know, Kemco, who developed the game, said, like, the computer kind of needs these extra units to make the game harder for you.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:22.640
Like, if you want scenario five to be our most challenging scenario, we need to give the computer more units.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:36.980
Yeah, and I think maybe, like, in terms of replayability, I do like that they added this feature so that maybe not removing units, but you can switch up how the computer, like, what their team makeup is like.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:48.380
Like, oh, let's see if we give them more bombers instead of more anti-aircraft, and let's see if I have less, like, if I change that in a similar way.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:52.200
And so you can have a bunch of different extra scenarios that you've crafted yourself.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:59.600
But, yeah, I don't think that they really wanted us to mess with it to make it easier.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:01.640
That just kind of defeats the point.

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:06.100
And the good news is you can change your loadouts.

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:11.460
Yes. And that's probably, as a consequence, why you can change the computer loadouts with the Player 2 controller.

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:14.820
They probably just program the same setup as the two-player game.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:23.020
But it's very important that you can change your loadout, because I think that would make these scenarios really suck and not make them good for replayability.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:30.580
If you were always forced to be like, well, in this scenario, you know, you're all tanks or something like that.

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:32.780
It's like, that's up for the player to decide.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:39.180
But the fact that you get to change your loadout before the scenario, I found it to be incredibly helpful.

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:45.180
I also found it as a way to figure out, like, you know, which ways are the easiest to exploit the computer.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:48.140
Well, I mean, yeah, that's at the core of strategy.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:57.500
Like, you're trying to optimize, and I think if they were to handicap you there and say, like, no, this is just, this is your makeup.

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:01.040
Like, this is going to be how many planes you have and how many tanks you have.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:05.100
You're starting to err into the side of, like, a puzzle game.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:28.140
Because I think one very specific feature of strategy games is the ability to also alter the starting conditions within reason and provide a way to change your team makeup and all those fun pregame situations.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:31.300
So I appreciate that they added that.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:37.200
But I'm sure that strategy outside of the NES is pretty well developed by now.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:43.540
I don't know too much about 80s strategy video games, but I'm just glad that they included that.

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:48.580
And that isn't, like, a newer feature that was just found in the 90s or 2000s.

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:58.500
Right, right. And to be fair to your point there, computer turn-based strategy games are not only a thing, but they're a lot more developed than Desert Commander was at the time.

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:05.000
Desert Commander was favorably reviewed for the NES, but was just seen as, like, an introductory game to turn-based strategy.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.060
Yeah, good for the NES. Right, right.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:10.220
Or maybe just good for console gaming in general.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:13.700
You know, you have to remember, home computers are very expensive in 1989.

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:20.340
You know, just because, like, there was a lot of home computer video games, those were mostly just being made by passionate people.

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:29.160
I don't think, like, you know, Nintendo ever thought that the family computer, the Famicom, was eventually going to become a home computer.

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:32.520
I think they always saw it as a video game console first, you know?

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:35.540
Yeah, for sure. So I was talking about changing the loadouts.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:39.240
And I guess what I really want to talk about here is just units and balance.

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:45.400
We don't have to give an overview of all the different unit types that you can have and, you know, who beats what.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:50.500
It is kind of a rock, paper, scissors thing where some units are strong to others and weaker to others.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:54.520
And that's important. Every game kind of needs that as the basics.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:57.220
Otherwise, one unit would just be the de facto.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:10.660
But I did find the bombers in this game, especially because of the overall objective of taking out the headquarters, I did find the bombers to just be something that I always wanted to stock up more than anything.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:18.800
Sean, curious if you found something similar or if you thought there was a particular unit that was just useless, you know, talk away.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.360
Yeah, bombers are definitely very useful.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:31.580
I kind of I would sort of take away some of my fighters and just replace those with bombers.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:47.160
I didn't get too crazy with customization, but I did figure that I'll just overpower on the offensive side and try and like I don't really need to shoot down their bombers if they're not going to use them very well because computer.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:50.300
If I was playing against you, I might double think that.

00:18:52.040 --> 00:19:13.300
But yeah, I didn't play too much with the loadout just because I think that while there is that rock, paper, scissors plus mentality here, it's still possible as a unit at a disadvantage to do some damage.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:30.460
Like it's it's it's like they it's balanced a little too much in that even when you're supposed to be at a disadvantage, you can still you can still like use a fighter against a ground troop and you might take a few out.

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:43.560
Like I thought that I figured like if if they're going to do that, it's kind of taking away from the the uniqueness and the utility of each kind of unit.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:54.760
Obviously, there are more utility units like the anti air is going to be much better against aircraft and it's going to be against like a ground artillery.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:20:02.860
But I think they should have made their specializations even stronger than they were in this game.

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:04.640
Yeah, because I mean, that's a good point.

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.240
There's you know, there's terrain advantages.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:10.120
There's advantages of air versus land.

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:13.620
There's advantages to like where you can go on the map.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:24.540
Right. I mean, you know, in the earliest stage, it's very easy to just kind of park your aircrafts over the ocean where nobody can really touch them and then just swoop in for the attack.

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:27.220
There's a lot there's a lot of things like that.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:40.460
But there's no there's no like special bonus for like or a particular thing for each unit that like they can pull is like a finishing move, if you will, or like a special extra bonus.

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:44.100
I think there's something on the menu for each unit that said power.

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:46.200
And I thought like, oh, wow, that's really cool.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:49.460
Like each one has its own power up or special ability.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:55.440
And then when you click on that, I think it just tells you literally like how much ammo you have left, like how many units.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:57.820
You're thinking of it like Final Fantasy or something.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:10.040
Right, right. And I just thought that was like, oh, man, there was a chance there for like, you know, like once per, you know, per scenario, the tank can just like fire a really amazing shot.

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:17.240
That's like guaranteed to take out minimum three units, you know, like just things like that would have been interesting to see added here.

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:28.600
Because otherwise, I think what you wind up with is playing a scenario once, seeing the computer's hand and being like, well, you know, he doesn't really have any aircrafts at all.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:39.180
Yeah, this one. And so if I make a team of 12 bombers and, you know, like a tank to take out the anti-aircrafts, like I'm pretty much going to win this thing.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:41.960
And I think you see that a lot in this game.

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:46.300
I don't know if that, you know, again, we're talking about this, you know, strategy games being all about optimization.

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:53.760
I don't know what point it becomes optimization versus, you know, cheesing against the computer there.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:54.900
Yeah, it's a fine line.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:22.660
It has a strong, the map in general has a strong favor to those who wait.

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:26.300
And I'm sure that's not just for the computer game either.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:37.960
I would imagine that, you know, even on a human to human scale, if me and you played, we would both play a very tight defensive game waiting almost for the attack.

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:49.100
I feel like because of the size of these maps and because of how enemy movements work and stuff like that, you're kind of encouraged to be defensive.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:23:07.140
Yeah, and that's why like some of the modern like XCOM XCOM 2, for example, like they added turn timers to many fans dismay just because there was too much turtling.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:17.760
And it does make it a more interesting game when you have when you have to get something done in a time slash turn limit because you actually have to come out of that shell.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:20.620
And I think maybe that could have helped here.

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:32.040
I mean, it's kind of hard to contextualize if you're trying to make it a trying to make it realistic, like there was no time limit, but like maybe as like a supply limit that always sort of ticked down.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:39.660
Like we've seen in some other games that we always kind of complained about, but it may maybe makes more sense in a strategy game.

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:43.980
Just to put some pressure on you to actually do something.

00:23:45.260 --> 00:24:05.200
And you were also talking about the maps affecting this as well, because, yeah, like while the road network looks a little different and your spawn points change a little bit and spawn locations within your your forces will get split and things like that.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:29.980
But I think like if there was if they thought of it more as like an arena shooter where a different map means almost a completely different like philosophy of map making like they'll if they added like a level, for example, that was like a bridge or like a couple bridges and had some choke points that weren't just like, oh, here's some mountains.

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:31.080
You can drive over them.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:32.000
You can just take longer.

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:41.260
I think that could have helped this game's case of creating a bit more map variety and therefore gameplay variety.

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:45.680
Yeah. I don't know if it's because, you know, and I'm sure you had a similar experience.

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:57.860
I don't know if it's because we played turn based strategy games before, but I feel like, you know, for a beginner's game, the obvious mistake here to make is like going into the very first scenario.

00:24:57.860 --> 00:25:03.260
And just spreading out your unit to cross the map to be like, all right, I'm covered everywhere.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:09.560
You know, like when you're covered everywhere, you're covered nowhere because it just takes one unit to take down yours.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:12.760
And now you have a whole, you know, expose to you.

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:22.540
I don't know how they could have solved that in this game, given how basic it is, especially, you know, just with the one objective.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:30.460
Like it's never just like, you know, don't have this many casualties or also take out all the tanks or something like that.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:33.840
Like because it is just a one objective game, I'm not sure what they could have done there.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:50.040
I really like your suggestion, not even just for bridges, but just like it's a shame that there's, you know, there's rocks, there's water, there's roads, and they don't really have like they're really just on the map for color more than anything else.

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.560
Like, yes, only the planes can go over the water.

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:57.940
But I don't know if you like noticed anything different, but the water is only on the outsides anyway.

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:04.640
So it's not like there's this big, you know, moat that we have to cross over that only your planes will have access to.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:08.360
It's basically just hidden in the background and, you know, use it if you want to.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:10.000
The computer won't really take advantage of it.

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:13.520
Or it's, you know, wow, the rocks would be like the mountains.

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:15.600
Like no tanks can go over those.

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:16.660
But it's like, no, they can.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:18.300
They just don't move as fast.

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.520
It's like, no, they just shouldn't be able to go over those, right?

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:22.820
Yeah, I agree.

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:27.600
Like that adds to like the specialization that I was talking about.

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:35.860
If there are some units that literally can't go over a terrain, I guess they already did water.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:38.480
But that's the most basic, I guess.

00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:41.480
But that extends more.

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:50.620
It also extends over to like the special terrain areas that they have here.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:55.780
Like there's some walls that add like a defensive bonus, I believe.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:58.040
I think they're supposed to be like bunkers for you.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:07.260
Yeah, like it's a defensive bonus, but they're just so scattered and like only one unit can take advantage of it at once.

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:18.140
It sort of reminded me of the little fortresses in Fire Emblem that it was kind of silly to me that they were just in random spots.

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:21.200
They didn't really like add to choke points or anything.

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:22.520
It was sort of the same thing.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:27.080
And then you have the airfields and supply depots.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:40.700
But they don't really add to the landscape so much as like, oh, here is one place that whether I have one or the other, like it's not really adding to the landscape.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:47.920
It's just I'm having trouble like actually saying this, articulating myself here.

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:57.880
No, I think I know what you mean because ultimately these things, right, aren't put strategically in a way that they matter any more than at the beginning of the game, right?

00:27:57.940 --> 00:28:05.200
Like you're always near an oasis and a bunker and, you know, and the roads to get around.

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:09.700
But like the roads are really like, yes, they're great for movement, but they're really out of the way.

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:11.800
There's no like great roads out there.

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:14.700
So you would really spread your units out by using the roads.

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:24.480
And then the oasis, it's like, you know, if you're coming to them or you're going to meet them in the middle or something like that, or they're coming to you, maybe I guess is like the only situation where the oasis matters.

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:31.500
But like these things aren't really strategically placed in a way other than the airports, which I think are important.

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:32.800
They're not airports.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:33.880
They're like runways or whatever.

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:38.880
Yeah, the airfields. Those are important, you know, because that's how you refuel your planes.

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:43.320
And the towns are important to recollect supplies.

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:46.520
So I think like, you know, there's a mix there, right?

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:50.600
It's like I didn't really see the benefits of the oasis or the bunkers that much.

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:57.800
I'm sure they would have played a role if maybe the maps were smaller and there was less like real estate and you want to make sure you have those.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:15.600
Yeah. If they contributed to like area denial, like if you being in this one spot and holding your position here made it so that the opposite side would have a harder time even operating in that area because it gives you a boost to like attack range or something along those lines.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:19.380
I think that's what I was that's what I'm sort of getting at.

00:29:19.540 --> 00:29:54.200
Yeah. You know, again, you know, against the and maybe that's because now you have like the element of surprise or whatever.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:30:04.900
But I think I would have liked to have actually seen it as a more, you know, a more tactical battle where it's like, you know, it doesn't matter if you're the attacker or the defender.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:07.220
It's it's purely unit versus unit.

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:09.160
I think so.

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:14.900
What I do think about that is I think it does kind of make sense that the initiator would have an edge.

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:37.660
And that's simply because your damage in this in this simple way, your damage is literally just like you get and you get a number of attacks or potential hits based on how many units are left in that squadron or however much health you have left on that unit.

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:39.760
You can look at it both ways.

00:30:39.760 --> 00:31:00.280
So if you have 10, if it's a full platoon, you have 10, you will do 10 attacks and they may hit or miss, but you do those attacks and whatever the enemy, on the other hand, they only get to do as many attacks as they have units left.

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:13.900
So since you are attacking first, you are already making their offense less capable by killing those units and making them do less attacks at a time.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:20.160
So there definitely is an advantage to the to the initiator.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:23.100
But I do think it kind of works out.

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:25.020
It's just it's just very simple.

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:37.580
And that's really the only that's the only thing adding like any modifier minus the rock, paper, scissors, how useful that unit would be.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:39.500
If you if you get what I'm saying.

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:49.920
The last thing I really want to talk about in terms of the units here is that, you know, each of them has a ammo capacity and a fuel capacity.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:53.640
And in a longer game, this will matter.

00:31:53.640 --> 00:32:04.000
You will run out of ammo or run out of fuel if you don't, you know, if you basically just try to be as offensive as possible and keep taking out unit after unit after unit.

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:06.860
I feel like that's very necessary.

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:21.760
Not only from like a simulation war style game where like obviously you wouldn't just have infinite ammo and infinite fuel, but also just from like a, you know, if we're not talking about the computer, we're just talking about human to human.

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:23.600
Like game balance. That is right.

00:32:23.780 --> 00:32:30.360
Like game balance. Like that is an interesting thing that you could be like on the cusp of victory.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:36.540
But all of your, you know, all of your nearby units don't have any ammo and suddenly have to retreat.

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:43.120
And now, you know, the headquarters has like time to go back and get supplies and, you know, reinforce themselves.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:51.160
Like I feel like it didn't happen so much for me with the computer, but it would come into play a lot more in a head to head game.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:33:10.440
Yeah, like if we were playing and you were kind of lax with your placement of your supply trucks, I could just go after them and just try and wait you out and see you run out of ammo and maybe try and guard the places on the map that you can get supplies.

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:14.380
Like that's definitely a valid strategy.

00:33:16.520 --> 00:33:22.240
And I do like that there is that purely unit to unit strategy.

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:32.400
I guess I would have just liked a bit more, a bit more of it tied into the map, which is what we've been talking about.

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:36.400
Yep, yep. One last thing on the map too, now that I'm remembering it.

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:46.460
You know, infantry units, the ground foot soldiers, there's, they're almost useless without the, what are they?

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:47.520
The transport, yeah.

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:48.800
Yeah, the transports.

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:50.160
They're almost useless without those.

00:33:50.300 --> 00:33:55.500
But then you have to almost think of the opportunity cost there of, you know, it takes two units now.

00:33:55.500 --> 00:34:03.920
You have to have the truck and the soldiers when you could just be applying those to like anti-aircrafts or bombers or something like that.

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:14.140
I really found myself, you know, as I continue to play this game more, just ditching a lot of the field units outside of the armored cars and the tanks.

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:16.380
Did you see, did you know something similar?

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:18.980
Were you finding a good use for your infantry?

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:31.180
Well, like I said, like I was, I was a bit more conservative in how much I changed their loadout just because I wanted to play it as I expected them to want me to.

00:34:31.720 --> 00:34:44.460
So I did, I did realize that, yeah, this, this transport is taking away a slot that could be used for a much more pack of punch unit.

00:34:45.700 --> 00:35:02.500
But I did think that if you're trying to be a sneaky boy and like you, your strategy is to just sort of wait for their units to kind of leave an opening for you to go and attack their, their base.

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:11.280
Having that transport that can move a pretty good distance, whereas your infantry really can't and your infantry being very good against the base.

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:27.360
I did see that, that sort of cost benefit analysis working in favor of keeping a transport just so you could do that little sleight of hand and get behind enemy lines and take care of it there.

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:35.400
You're really making me want to start looking for like the eSports Desert Commander series, you know, like I, what was it?

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:44.160
I just sent you the other day, we found out that Microsoft Excel has a best of 16 eSports competition happening as we're recording or whatever, right?

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:49.000
But like, I don't know, isn't this like the whole like hook of speed running and stuff like that?

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:53.520
Like niche games being, you know, seeing them be done in a way that you've never imagined them to be done.

00:35:53.520 --> 00:36:01.920
Like I would really like to see what a, you know, like with an ELO rating system, how like people would play Desert Commander.

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:04.080
I don't think there would ever be a scene big enough for it.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:17.740
But it's a shame that when you go look at like YouTube playthroughs or strategy guides or whatever, most of them are just suggesting the fastest victory rather than what you were going for, the practical victory with the units that they give you.

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:21.080
So since I did the same thing, I wanted to win.

00:36:21.440 --> 00:36:25.960
I'm curious, Sean, what were your thoughts on the scenarios with the default loadouts?

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:36.660
Did you, did you think that that was maybe a little unbalanced that they were, that they weren't giving you the units you needed or the, or the, you know, the amount of units you needed?

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:42.300
Or did you feel like, you know, still, it's just a matter of being defensive and waiting for your opportunity to strike?

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:55.080
Yeah, I mean, I wouldn't say that I was, I was playing it, it kind of was false to say that I was playing it the way they wanted me to play it because I was still just like kiting enemies because I had unlimited time.

00:36:56.120 --> 00:37:02.540
I can, I can, I can sort of turtle and just let, okay, this, this one artillery is kind of left alone.

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:10.660
If I get to him, his, his buddies won't be able to really split my, split my guys up too much.

00:37:10.820 --> 00:37:16.380
